From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shirogane <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] usb: musb: do not flush a packet that arrived before its
 request

musb_ep_restart() flushes the RX FIFO unconditionally when a request is
queued on an OUT endpoint. That destroys a packet which arrived while no
request was pending: musb_g_rx() has already returned early on finding an
empty request list, consuming the only interrupt the packet will ever
get, so nothing will ever pull it out again. The request then sits on the
queue waiting for an interrupt that cannot come, and the read blocks
forever.

Gadgets that always keep a request queued never notice. FunctionFS with
synchronous read() does: between returning from write() on the IN
endpoint and issuing the next read() on the OUT endpoint there is a
sub-millisecond gap, and a host that pipelines its next request into that
gap loses it. On the ePass boards this hangs the epass function on the
first exchange after SET_CONFIGURATION and on roughly every request that
follows a reply.

Check RXPKTRDY first: if a packet is already in the FIFO hand the request
to rxstate() so it gets picked up. 5.4 called rxstate() here
unconditionally, which is why the same userspace works on the F1C boards.

Do not flush in the other case either. The datasheet allows writing
FLUSHFIFO only while RXPKTRDY is set, and on the D1s the write is not
harmless when it races a reception in progress: with the host streaming
bulk OUT data, a flush issued between packets occasionally corrupts the
FIFO pointers, and the next packet then reports RXCOUNT=0 while
RXPKTRDY and FIFOFULL stay set. rxstate() believes the zero, completes
the request with actual=0 and the packet is lost, hanging large
transfers a few times per megabyte. An empty FIFO needs no flushing,
and a packet mid-flight raises its own interrupt once complete, so the
whole write is unnecessary; 5.4 never had it. Verified on the ePass
Next: 14/14 4MB put/get round-trips with matching md5, and the
RXCOUNT=0 state never reappears in over 3000 packet-before-request
windows, where before it occurred within every 4MB transfer.
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c
index 016d3f3fc..16ae90b58 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c
@@ -1171,9 +1171,26 @@ void musb_ep_restart(struct musb *musb, struct musb_request *req)
 		txstate(musb, req);
 	} else {
 		csr = musb_readw(epio, MUSB_RXCSR);
-		csr |= MUSB_RXCSR_FLUSHFIFO | MUSB_RXCSR_P_WZC_BITS;
-		musb_writew(epio, MUSB_RXCSR, csr);
-		musb_writew(epio, MUSB_RXCSR, csr);
+		if (csr & MUSB_RXCSR_RXPKTRDY) {
+			/*
+			 * A packet beat the request here: it is already sitting
+			 * in the FIFO, and the interrupt that delivered it has
+			 * been consumed by musb_g_rx() finding no request. No
+			 * further interrupt is coming for it, so flushing would
+			 * drop the packet for good and leave this request
+			 * waiting forever. Hand it to rxstate() instead.
+			 */
+			rxstate(musb, req);
+		}
+		/*
+		 * No FLUSHFIFO when RXPKTRDY is clear: the datasheet allows
+		 * writing FLUSHFIFO only while RXPKTRDY is set, and on this
+		 * IP a flush racing an in-progress reception corrupts the
+		 * FIFO pointers, making the next packet report RXCOUNT=0
+		 * while RXPKTRDY/FIFOFULL stay set. An empty FIFO needs no
+		 * flushing anyway; a packet mid-flight raises its own
+		 * interrupt once complete.
+		 */
 	}
 }
 
